---
title: Primitive arrays with stable row identity
description: Add, reorder and remove rows of a flat string array while keeping native, JavaScript-free submission working.
---

<script>
	import { Callout } from "$lib/markdown";
</script>

An array of primitives (`string[]`, `number[]`) submits fine as repeated flat input names — no JSON encoding needed — but adding, reordering and removing rows breaks naive `{#each}` keys. The fix is a separate list of row ids that you mutate in lockstep with the data.

## Schema

```ts title="src/routes/urls/schema.ts"
import { z } from "zod/v3";

export const urlsSchema = z.object({
	urls: z.array(z.string().url("Enter a valid URL.")).min(1),
});

export type UrlsData = z.infer<typeof urlsSchema>;
```

## Server

Nothing special: the repeated `urls[0]`, `urls[1]`, … input names parse into the array directly.

```ts title="src/routes/urls/+page.server.ts"
import { fail } from "@sveltejs/kit";
import { superValidate } from "sveltekit-superforms";
import { zod } from "sveltekit-superforms/adapters";
import { urlsSchema } from "./schema.js";

export const load = async () => ({
	form: await superValidate(
		{ urls: ["https://a.example", "https://b.example"] },
		zod(urlsSchema)
	),
});

export const actions = {
	default: async ({ request }) => {
		const form = await superValidate(request, zod(urlsSchema));
		return form.valid ? { form } : fail(400, { form });
	},
};
```

## Form

`ElementField` with the indexed name `urls[N]` renders the repeated parent name for native submission. The `{#each}` key is `rowIds[index]`, never the row value or object identity.

```svelte title="src/routes/urls/+page.svelte"
<script lang="ts">
	import { untrack } from "svelte";
	import { superForm, type SuperValidated } from "sveltekit-superforms";
	import { Control, ElementField, Fieldset, Label, Legend } from "@emmorts/formsnap";
	import type { UrlsData } from "./schema.js";

	let { data }: { data: { form: SuperValidated<UrlsData> } } = $props();

	const form = superForm(
		untrack(() => data.form),
		{ resetForm: false }
	);
	const { form: formData } = form;

	// Superforms replaces row objects when taint changes, so object identity is not a stable
	// key. Initialize row ids once from the initial row count.
	const initialRowCount = untrack(() => data.form.data.urls.length);
	let rowIds = $state(Array.from({ length: initialRowCount }, (_, index) => index));
	let nextRowId = initialRowCount;

	function addRow() {
		rowIds = [...rowIds, nextRowId++];
		$formData.urls = [...$formData.urls, ""];
	}

	function reorderRows() {
		rowIds = [...rowIds].reverse();
		$formData.urls = [...$formData.urls].reverse();
	}

	function removeRow() {
		rowIds = rowIds.slice(0, -1);
		$formData.urls = $formData.urls.slice(0, -1);
	}
</script>

<form method="POST">
	<Fieldset {form} name="urls" fieldErrors>
		<Legend>Website URLs</Legend>
		{#snippet description()}Enter one URL per row.{/snippet}
		{#each $formData.urls as _, index (rowIds[index])}
			<ElementField {form} name={`urls[${index}]` as `urls[${number}]`} fieldErrors>
				<Control>
					{#snippet children({ props })}
						<Label>URL {index + 1}</Label>
						<input {...props} bind:value={$formData.urls[index]} />
					{/snippet}
				</Control>
			</ElementField>
		{/each}
	</Fieldset>

	<button type="button" onclick={addRow}>Add row</button>
	<button type="button" onclick={reorderRows}>Reorder rows</button>
	<button type="button" onclick={removeRow}>Remove row</button>
	<button type="submit">Save URLs</button>
</form>
```

## How it works

- The form has no `use:enhance` and no `dataType: "json"`: the browser posts the repeated `urls[N]` names natively, and Superforms parses them back into the array. Row management buttons need JavaScript, but submission does not.
- `rowIds[index]` keys the `{#each}` block, so add, reorder and remove move the correct DOM nodes instead of re-binding every input.
- Both arrays are updated together in every mutation — `rowIds` and `$formData.urls` always stay the same length in the same order.
- `fieldErrors` on each `ElementField` renders per-row messages (an invalid URL), while `fieldErrors` on the `Fieldset` surfaces the array-level `min(1)` error.

<Callout type="warning">

Do not key rows by their value or by the row object. Duplicate values collide as keys, and Superforms swaps row objects during taint updates — both make Svelte reuse the wrong DOM node, scrambling input bindings and focus.

</Callout>

<Callout type="note">

This recipe is the JavaScript-free alternative to [nested data](/docs/recipes/nested-data). The moment rows become objects (`urls[N].label`), flat names can no longer express the shape and you need JSON submission.

</Callout>
